  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nIn the [import map section](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#import-maps), there's an example that says:\r\n\r\n> An import map can remap a class of module specifiers into a class of URLs by using trailing slashes, like so:\r\n> \r\n> ```json\r\n> {\r\n>   \"imports\": {\r\n>     \"moment/\": \"/node_modules/moment/src/\"\r\n>   }\r\n> }\r\n> ```\r\n>\r\n> This enables statements like `import localeData from \"moment/locale/zh-cn.js\";` to work, fetching and evaluating the JavaScript module at the `/node_modules/moment/src/moment/locale/zh-cn.js` URL.\r\n\r\nShouldn't the resolution be `/node_modules/moment/src/locale/zh-cn.js`? (Only one `moment` segment, not two.) Since the `moment/` part of the `moment/locale/zh-cn.js` module specifier is what matches the map, so it's replaced by `/node_modules/moment/src/`, and the remainder (`locale/zh-cn.js`) is then appended. At least, that's how steps 1.2.3 and 1.2.5 in [resolve an imports match](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#resolving-an-imports-match) read:\r\n\r\n> 1. For each *specifierKey* → *resolutionResult* of *specifierMap*:\r\n>    2. If all of the following are true:\r\n>      * *specifierKey* ends with U+002F (/);\r\n>      * *specifierKey* is a code unit prefix of *normalizedSpecifier*; and\r\n>      * either *asURL* is null, or *asURL* is special,\r\n>     then:\r\n>      3. Let *afterPrefix* be the portion of *normalizedSpecifier* after the initial *specifierKey* prefix.\r\n>      4. ...\r\n>      5. Let *url* be the result of URL parsing *afterPrefix* with *resolutionResult*.\r\n\r\nHappy to provide a PR if I'm not mistaken about this, though it's such a small edit that may be overkill.",
